WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.508
- Hello.

00:00:01.508 --> 00:00:02.341
- Hi.

00:00:02.341 --> 00:00:03.360
I am so excited to meet you.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:04.193
- Oh, really?

00:00:04.193 --> 00:00:05.280
- Yes, so excited.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.420
- [Kelly] Oh, I hope I don't let you down.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:07.643
- I love your music and you.

00:00:07.643 --> 00:00:08.534
- Okay.

00:00:08.534 --> 00:00:09.540
I was like, well meet Djimon.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:10.530
- It's so great to meet you, too.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:11.561
- Nice to meet you.

00:00:11.561 --> 00:00:13.080
- Miranda.
- Absolutely, yeah.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.000
- So what do you love about
Mission Unstoppable, though?

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.140
This is really cool what you're doing.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.310
This is with Geena Davis, right?

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:18.780
- Yes, it's just, it's really fun.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:20.340
It's my first time hosting a show.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.220
It's all about bringing
STEM to young girls

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.060
and letting them know

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.400
that it's actually something
they can be a part of.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.534
- Yeah.

00:00:27.534 --> 00:00:28.455
(audience cheers)

00:00:28.455 --> 00:00:29.538
Seeing yourself;

00:00:29.538 --> 00:00:30.371
that's like what you were
talking about with your son,

00:00:30.371 --> 00:00:32.220
like seeing yourself and
then you can achieve it.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.300
Same thing for young girls,

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.160
that people don't really drill that in,

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.060
like science and everything.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.790
As a host, do you feel like
you learn stuff as well?

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:39.623
'Cause I--
- All the time.

00:00:39.623 --> 00:00:40.456
- As a host for this show,

00:00:40.456 --> 00:00:41.880
I'm like, I learn stuff all the time.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.560
I think that's like the
coolest part of the job.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:44.393
- Yeah.

00:00:44.393 --> 00:00:45.750
I didn't even realize, it really,

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.880
even though I'm an adult
and the show is for kids,

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.840
it's made me love science in
a way that I never did before.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:52.980
So, it kind of did for me

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.570
what it's supposed to do for the viewers.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.520
- Yeah, and I know it's for children,

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.750
but I bet you most adults,

00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.070
it's like Smarter Than A Fifth Grader.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.720
I bet you most of us don't know.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.523
- I definitely feel like that when I'm,

00:01:02.523 --> 00:01:04.255
when I'm hosting.

00:01:04.255 --> 00:01:05.088
I'm like, I never knew any of this.

00:01:05.088 --> 00:01:06.104
- Yeah.

00:01:06.104 --> 00:01:06.937
I think it's really cool.

00:01:06.937 --> 00:01:08.070
It is interesting though,
later in life how you get more

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:09.600
interested in the history channel.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.280
You get more interested in science.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:12.270
It's almost backwards.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.103
When you're a kid,

00:01:13.103 --> 00:01:14.610
all you wanna do is run
outside and adventure

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:16.290
and it's almost like backwards.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:17.730
I don't know why, how we fix that.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:18.960
I don't know.
- Yeah.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.690
- So, Miranda, I heard
you had a very specific

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.400
favorite science subject as a kid.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.233
- Yes.

00:01:24.233 --> 00:01:25.740
So when I was a kid,
I was super into bugs,

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:28.300
which everyone thought was
weird when I was little.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:29.133
- That's not weird, yeah.

00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:29.966
- I was obsessed with bugs.

00:01:29.966 --> 00:01:31.140
I loved doing experiments.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.394
I would always get, you
know, the butterflies

00:01:33.394 --> 00:01:34.227
how you watch them.
- Yeah.

00:01:34.227 --> 00:01:35.060
- Go from caterpillars, turn in--

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:36.628
- [Kelly] Yes, metamorphosis, yeah.

00:01:36.628 --> 00:01:37.461
- And this is my millipede.

00:01:37.461 --> 00:01:38.294
I had a millipede for five years.

00:01:38.294 --> 00:01:40.140
- [Kelly] No, like you
were really into bugs.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.360
- I was into it.
- Yeah.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.560
- Millie the millipede.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.150
- That is, Millie.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.620
Oh, you shoulda had another one, Vanilli.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:47.453
Oh my God.

00:01:47.453 --> 00:01:48.630
Oh my God, that's amazing.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:49.860
So wait, you're younger,

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.150
you're too young for that joke.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.520
I hear that you're obsessed
with cats now, right?

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:54.353
- [Miranda] Yes.

00:01:54.353 --> 00:01:55.500
- So you can't have that
around your house then.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.270
- I love animals.
- They'll eat it.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.460
- I'm always trying to get
them to do animal segments

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.350
on Mission Unstoppable
on the science show.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:02.183
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:02.183 --> 00:02:03.016
- Constantly I'm always like,

00:02:03.016 --> 00:02:04.050
anything that has to do with animals.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.239
But, yeah.
- You so do love animals.

00:02:06.239 --> 00:02:09.690
- I love cats and I foster
cats and I recently,

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.220
'cause it's been raining a lot in L.A.,

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.940
I recently, a cat appeared
on my porch in the rain

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.680
and I thought I was doing this great thing

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.180
by taking it to a rescue

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:19.710
and then I found out it had a microchip

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:21.510
and lived like a block from my house.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:22.343
- Oh.

00:02:22.343 --> 00:02:23.993
You like, took someone's cat.

00:02:23.993 --> 00:02:24.826
- Exactly.

00:02:24.826 --> 00:02:25.860
So I had to go back to the
rescue, pick the cat up,

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.550
and like awkwardly bring
it back to its family.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.787
So I basically just stole a cat.

00:02:31.787 --> 00:02:32.790
- You stole a cat.
- Yeah.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:33.810
- That's incredible.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:34.800
Okay, well other than that,

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.480
while you're doing Mission Unstoppable,

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:37.830
what's the coolest science fact

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.150
that you've found from the show?

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.280
- Well, I've experienced
sleep paralysis before.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.290
I don't know if that's
ever happened to you guys.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:44.448
- What does that mean?

00:02:44.448 --> 00:02:45.450
- It's where you wake up,

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.130
your eyes are open and
you're lying in bed,

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:48.060
but you're asleep.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.460
So, your dreams that you're having

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.380
seem like they're happening
in the room you're in,

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.630
like your eyes are open
but you can't move.

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:56.040
- That, that's terrifying.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.600
Have you ever experienced that or do you?

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.220
- I've experienced something similar,

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.430
but I'm not sure if it's quite the same.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.614
But yeah, there's something--

00:03:04.614 --> 00:03:05.447
- What similar?

00:03:05.447 --> 00:03:06.280
Like insomnia, or?

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:08.610
- Well, I'm not quite
sure, but it seems like,

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:13.533
yeah, I was asleep briefly,
but I was wide awake.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:18.390
My eyes are open, I'm yelling to the sky.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:21.690
I'm completely paralyzed in
the bed and can't move and--

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:23.700
- No, I think that's kind of exactly

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:24.638
like what she just said.

00:03:24.638 --> 00:03:25.830
- That's it, that's exactly it.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:26.663
- Yeah, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:03:26.663 --> 00:03:28.475
- It sounds terrifying, yeah.

00:03:28.475 --> 00:03:29.308
- Yeah, it's so scary

00:03:29.308 --> 00:03:31.560
and we did this whole segment
on it and it was crazy.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.810
They're talking about how
the best thing you can do

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:35.940
is have a really set sleep schedule

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:37.500
and try to really stick to it.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:38.333
'Cause it usually happens

00:03:38.333 --> 00:03:40.110
when you're stressed or really tired.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:40.943
- Oh my God.

00:03:40.943 --> 00:03:42.810
That's the, it's like
the most annoying thing

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:43.740
a doctor can tell me.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:44.573
- [Miranda] I know.

00:03:44.573 --> 00:03:45.406
Try to sleep perfectly.

00:03:45.406 --> 00:03:46.239
- Are you stressed?

00:03:46.239 --> 00:03:47.280
And I'm like, am I breathing?

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:48.113
I'm like--
- Yeah.

00:03:48.113 --> 00:03:49.470
- There's a lot on the plate.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:52.680
I'm like, yeah, there's no
button for it, but okay.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:53.640
But I've never experienced that,

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:54.600
so maybe I'm doing all right.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:55.980
- Yeah, I think you're doing good.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.620
- Because if I were, yeah,
I think that would, I think,

00:03:58.620 --> 00:03:59.760
God knows I can't handle that.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.163
So you're turning, is
it 30, in a few weeks?

00:04:02.163 --> 00:04:03.120
- Yes, I am.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:04.534
- [Kelly] That's exciting.

00:04:04.534 --> 00:04:05.367
(audience cheers)

00:04:05.367 --> 00:04:06.200
- Thank you.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:08.280
- My twenties, I felt like
were really hard, although fun.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:09.240
There were great moments,

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:10.650
but my thirties were like way better.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:11.940
Are you excited about your thirties?

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:13.329
- I am excited about my thirties.

00:04:13.329 --> 00:04:14.162
- [Kelly] Yeah?

00:04:14.162 --> 00:04:14.995
- I hear that from everybody that

00:04:14.995 --> 00:04:16.140
it's even better than the
twenties, so I'm ready.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.510
- And honestly now I'm, I
just turned 40 this past year.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:19.770
So like, 40 is the same thing.

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:21.360
I think it, I don't know how you feel,

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.482
but I feel like life gets
better the older I get.

00:04:23.482 --> 00:04:24.315
- Oh, for sure.

00:04:24.315 --> 00:04:25.148
- Maybe you mature or something.

00:04:25.148 --> 00:04:25.981
- For sure.
- Yeah.

00:04:25.981 --> 00:04:28.830
- Maturity makes you
enjoy and relax about--

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.090
- Wiser about things.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:31.500
- Yes.
- Yeah.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:32.970
Do you have any plans for your birthday?

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.700
- I am actually going to be
in Thailand for my birthday.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:36.806
- [Kelly] What?

00:04:36.806 --> 00:04:38.356
- For the first time
ever, I've never been.

00:04:38.356 --> 00:04:39.450
- For work or pleasure, or both?

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.550
- It's for a Netflix movie, but my,

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:43.440
some of my friends are gonna
fly out for my birthday,

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:44.640
so, I'm so excited.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:45.473
- That's so cool.

00:04:45.473 --> 00:04:46.306
Have you ever been to Thailand?

00:04:46.306 --> 00:04:47.139
- I've never been before.

00:04:47.139 --> 00:04:48.150
I'm going to Phuket.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:49.230
- Oh, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:50.340
- I love Thailand.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:51.270
Have you ever been to Thailand?

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:53.010
- Yes, I filmed there once.

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:54.180
- It's really, really cool.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:55.013
I love it.

00:04:55.013 --> 00:04:55.846
The little markets.

00:04:55.846 --> 00:04:57.390
- Did you stay for a while when you went?

00:04:57.390 --> 00:04:58.223
- I did.

00:04:58.223 --> 00:04:59.580
I'm always, it's always around work.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.620
Like, it's like if I'm touring,
I think I was touring there

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.648
or doing like MTV Asia Awards.

00:05:03.648 --> 00:05:04.500
I can't remember what I was doing there.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.240
I've been there a few
times but I, but I love it.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.130
I love the people, they're so kind.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:10.110
I love, I think they call
them tuk-tuks or something.

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:11.325
Like they drive you--
- Tuk-tuk, yeah.

00:05:11.325 --> 00:05:12.158
- Tuk-tuk, tuk-tuk.

00:05:12.158 --> 00:05:12.991
That's what it is.

00:05:12.991 --> 00:05:13.950
They're in like the bicycles

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:14.790
and they drive you around everywhere.

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:16.775
That was the first time.
- Oh, cool.

00:05:16.775 --> 00:05:18.286
- I was like, I just like
saying, "Can I get a tuk-tuk?"

00:05:18.286 --> 00:05:19.119
Like I just like saying it.

00:05:19.119 --> 00:05:20.310
- I'm gonna say that as soon as I land.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:22.501
- Don't say, "took took"
'cause I mispronounced it.

00:05:22.501 --> 00:05:23.940
Tuk-tuk, okay.

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:25.860
Well it is great that
you're doing iCarly again.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:27.240
Did you think you would
ever do this again?

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:28.710
Like a reboot?

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:29.543
- I didn't.

00:05:29.543 --> 00:05:30.376
(audience cheers)

00:05:30.376 --> 00:05:31.209
- Yeah?

00:05:31.209 --> 00:05:32.042
It's such a popular show.

00:05:32.042 --> 00:05:33.090
- Yeah, it's been so much fun.

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:34.800
We just finished the third season

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.380
and there's this whole love story

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:38.640
that's been going on on the show

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:40.710
since I was 13 when I first started it

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:44.010
between my character Carly and
my friend Freddie on the show

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:47.490
and this whole season is all
about like, our love, so.

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:48.323
- I love that.

00:05:48.323 --> 00:05:49.440
- It's been a fun season and--

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:50.820
- What a cool thing with a cast

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:52.800
to like know since you were a kid, right?

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:53.633
And growing up?

00:05:53.633 --> 00:05:54.466
That's kind of cool.

00:05:54.466 --> 00:05:56.220
- Yeah, we really grew up
together, so it's crazy.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:57.960
Like Nathan, who plays
Freddie on the show,

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.540
has like two daughters now and is married

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:01.373
and it's just crazy.

00:06:01.373 --> 00:06:02.206
- So different.

00:06:02.206 --> 00:06:03.600
- Being with someone your whole life,

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:05.565
like hanging out with someone
like that, it's really nice.

00:06:05.565 --> 00:06:06.398
- Yeah.

00:06:06.398 --> 00:06:08.250
Well the original show
was for tweens and teens,

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.717
but do you ever get parents like saying,

00:06:10.717 --> 00:06:11.880
"Oh my God, I love your show"?

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.010
Because as a parent, I've
actually watched the show

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:16.530
with kiddos before and it's kind of cool

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:18.510
when you make a show that
parents can sit there

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:20.670
with their kids and watch
and you like it as well.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:22.500
You know, you're not like, oh this again.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:23.640
You know, like that's kind of cool.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:25.020
Do you ever hear from
parents, in that sense?

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:26.490
- Yeah, sometimes parents
will come up to me

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:29.280
and it's really nice
'cause now that the show

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:32.130
is for the original viewers
and they've all grown up.

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:32.963
- Yeah.

00:06:32.963 --> 00:06:34.477
- They, a lot of people will
come up to me and say like,

00:06:34.477 --> 00:06:37.837
"I'm 30 now and I've been
watching it with my dad again,"

00:06:37.837 --> 00:06:39.253
or like, "I've been watching
it with my mom again."

00:06:39.253 --> 00:06:40.464
- Yeah.
- And that feels really good,

00:06:40.464 --> 00:06:41.520
like, it's something they
did when they were young

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:43.652
and now they can do it
again with their family.

00:06:43.652 --> 00:06:44.880
- Yeah, you're getting,
helping people connect.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:46.930
- It's really nice.
- That's cool, yeah.

